--- (4) CANDIDATE LAWS (weighted LS in linear Phi; weights 1/SE^2) ---
    fit points: SPARC bins (non-empty, SE>0) + O01 primary + O01 deep tail, N = 14
    (a) Phi = c x^p :            c = 0.957 +- 0.057,  p = +0.154 +- 0.039  (z_p = +3.92),  chi2 = 18.9/12 = 1.58
    (b) Phi = 1 + alpha ln x :   c0 = 0.938 +- 0.053,  alpha = +0.115 +- 0.031  (z_alpha = +3.64),  chi2 = 19.7/12 = 1.64
    (c) Phi = (1+x)/(1+x/b) :    b = 0.492 +- 0.043  (family implies Phi(1) = 0.660; the constraint Phi(1)=1 forces b=1, the constant Phi=1, degenerate), chi2 = 76.6/13 = 5.89
    (c2) Phi = c_b(1+x)/(1+x/b), Phi(1)=1:  b = 3.391 +- 0.709  (deep Phi(0) = 0.647, Phi(inf) = 2.196),  chi2 = 18.1/13 = 1.39

--- (5) VERDICT (pre-registered rule) ---
    C1: 44.4% of 9 overlapping comparisons within 3 combined SE (need >= 2/3): FAIL
    C2: power-law exponent p/SE_p = +3.92 (need |z| > 5): FAIL
    VERDICT: The sign-mirror is a GENUINE SYSTEMATICS DIVIDE (registered finding): Phi(x) does NOT collapse across surveys at fixed x -- the same conclusion N05's arbitration program would reach, now data-side.
    BEST FIT: form (a);  Phi(x) = 0.957 x^+0.154  [p = +0.154 +- 0.039, chi2/dof = 1.58; fit on SPARC bins + O01 dwarfs, inverse-variance weighted].  Falsifier: any survey with >= 3 rings in one x-bin whose mean Phi departs > 3 combined SE from this curve (e.g., a MIGHTEE-class measurement at x ~ 0.03-0.1 that does not sit on Phi(x)); or a flat fit p with |p|/SE_p < 5 on the pooled SPARC+dwarf dataset.

